[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What's the scope of "multiple forms" for this work? → Extract + prove in tests (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Scope set: shared module + login rewired + a second field set proven in tests.

  Second question, on how expressive the rules should be. This is the interface everyone downstream lives with, and it's
  the expensive one to change later — once forms declare their rules in a given shape, changing the shape means
  touching every form.

  - [1mRequired-only[0m — matches today's behavior exactly ([38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m only checks presence). Smallest surface. But the
    second form you add will almost certainly want a min-length or an email check, and adding rule [3mtypes[0m later to a
    required-only design means reworking the declaration format.
  - [1mFixed built-in rule set[0m ([38;5;153mrequired[39m, [38;5;153mminLength[39m, [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m, [38;5;153mpattern[39m, [38;5;153memail[39m) — covers what login/signup forms actually
    need, no extensibility hooks. Adding a new built-in later is a one-line addition to the module; forms don't change.
  - [1mBuilt-ins + custom validator functions[0m — a rule can be an arbitrary [38;5;153m(value, allValues) => error | null[39m. Handles
    cross-field rules like "confirm password must match". Most flexible; also the only option where rules stop being
    plain data (can't be serialized or declared in JSON).

  I'd recommend the fixed built-in set. It's honestly sized for a login/signup app, rules stay plain data, and the
  escape hatch for custom functions can be added later without breaking any form that didn't need it — that's the
  cheap-to-change direction. Cross-field matching is the one thing it can't express; if password-confirm is on your near
  horizon, say so and I'd go with the third.
